# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction and notation {#introduction-and-notation}"
msgstr "Pengenalan dan tatatanda {#introduction-and-notation}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To use a quantum algorithm, classical data must somehow be brought into a quantum circuit. This is usually referred to as data *encoding*, but is also called data *loading*. Recall from previous lessons the notion of a feature mapping, a mapping of data features from one space to another. Just transferring classical data to a quantum computer is a sort of mapping, and could be called a feature mapping. In practice, the built-in feature mappings in Qiskit (like `z_feature_map` and `zz_feature_map`) will typically include rotation layers and entangling layers that extend the state to many dimensions in the Hilbert space. This encoding process is a critical part of quantum machine learning algorithms and directly affects their computational capabilities.\n"
msgstr "Untuk menggunakan algoritma kuantum, data klasik mesti dibawa masuk ke dalam litar kuantum dengan cara tertentu. Proses ini biasanya dirujuk sebagai *pengekodan* data, tetapi juga disebut sebagai *pemuatan* data. Ingat kembali dari pelajaran sebelumnya tentang konsep pemetaan ciri, iaitu pemetaan ciri data dari satu ruang ke ruang yang lain. Memindahkan data klasik ke komputer kuantum semata-mata adalah satu jenis pemetaan, dan boleh disebut sebagai pemetaan ciri. Dalam amalan, pemetaan ciri terbina dalam Qiskit (seperti `z_feature_map` dan `zz_feature_map`) biasanya akan menyertakan lapisan putaran dan lapisan pengelitan yang mengembangkan keadaan ke banyak dimensi dalam ruang Hilbert. Proses pengekodan ini merupakan bahagian kritikal algoritma pembelajaran mesin kuantum dan secara langsung mempengaruhi keupayaan pengiraan mereka.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some of the encoding techniques below can be efficiently classically simulated; this is particularly easy to see in encoding methods that yield product states (that is, they do not entangle qubits). And remember that quantum utility is most likely to lie where the quantum-like complexity of the dataset is well-matched by the encoding method. So it is very likely that you will end up writing your own encoding circuits. Here, we show a wide variety of possible encoding strategies simply so that you can compare and contrast them, and see what is possible. There are some very general statements that can be made about the usefulness of encoding techniques. For example, `efficient_su2` (see below) with a full entangling scheme is much more likely to capture quantum features of data than methods that yield product states  (like `z_feature_map`). But this does not mean `efficient_su2` is sufficient, or sufficiently well-matched to your dataset, to yield a quantum speed-up. That requires careful consideration of the structure of the data being modeled or classified. There is also a balancing act with circuit depth, since many feature maps which fully entangle the qubits in a circuit yield very deep circuits, too deep to get usable results on today's quantum computers.\n"
msgstr "Beberapa teknik pengekodan di bawah boleh disimulasikan dengan cekap secara klasik; ini amat jelas kelihatan dalam kaedah pengekodan yang menghasilkan keadaan produk (iaitu, yang tidak mengelitkan qubit). Dan ingat bahawa kegunaan kuantum kemungkinan besar terletak di mana kerumitan seperti kuantum dalam set data sepadan dengan baik dengan kaedah pengekodan. Jadi sangat mungkin anda akan menulis litar pengekodan sendiri. Di sini, kami menunjukkan pelbagai strategi pengekodan yang mungkin supaya anda boleh membandingkan dan membezakannya, serta melihat apa yang boleh dilakukan. Ada beberapa pernyataan umum yang boleh dibuat tentang kegunaan teknik pengekodan. Contohnya, `efficient_su2` (lihat di bawah) dengan skim pengelitan penuh jauh lebih berkemungkinan untuk menangkap ciri kuantum data berbanding kaedah yang menghasilkan keadaan produk (seperti `z_feature_map`). Tetapi ini tidak bermakna `efficient_su2` mencukupi, atau sepadan dengan set data anda, untuk menghasilkan peningkatan kelajuan kuantum. Perkara itu memerlukan pertimbangan teliti terhadap struktur data yang dimodel atau diklasifikasikan. Ada juga pertimbangan keseimbangan dengan kedalaman litar, kerana banyak peta ciri yang mengelitkan sepenuhnya semua qubit dalam litar menghasilkan litar yang sangat dalam, terlalu dalam untuk mendapat keputusan berguna pada komputer kuantum hari ini.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Notation {#notation}"
msgstr "Tatatanda {#notation}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A dataset is a set of $M$ data vectors: $\\text{X} = \\{\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\,|\\,j\\in [M]\\}$, where each vector is $N$ dimensional, that is, $\\vec{x}^{(j)}=(\\vec{x}^{(j)}_1,\\ldots,\\vec{x}^{(j)}_N)\\in\\mathbb{R}^N$. This could be extended to complex data features. In this lesson, we may occasionally use these notations for the full set $(\\text{X}),$ and its specific elements like $\\vec{x}^{(j)}$. But we will mostly refer to the loading of a single vector from our dataset at a time, and will often simply refer to a single vector of $N$ features as $\\vec{x}$.\n"
msgstr "Set data ialah set $M$ vektor data: $\\text{X} = \\{\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\,|\\,j\\in [M]\\}$, di mana setiap vektor berdimensi $N$, iaitu $\\vec{x}^{(j)}=(\\vec{x}^{(j)}_1,\\ldots,\\vec{x}^{(j)}_N)\\in\\mathbb{R}^N$. Ini boleh diperluaskan kepada ciri data kompleks. Dalam pelajaran ini, kami mungkin kadang-kadang menggunakan tatatanda ini untuk set penuh $(\\text{X}),$ dan elemen spesifiknya seperti $\\vec{x}^{(j)}$. Tetapi kami kebanyakannya akan merujuk kepada pemuatan satu vektor dari set data pada satu masa, dan sering merujuk kepada satu vektor tunggal dengan $N$ ciri sebagai $\\vec{x}$.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Additionally, it is common to use the symbol $\\Phi(\\vec{x})$ to refer to the feature mapping $\\Phi$ of data vector $\\vec{x}$. In quantum computing specifically, it is common to refer to mappings in quantum computing using $U(\\vec{x}),$ a notation that reinforces the unitary nature of these operations. One could correctly use the same symbol for both; both are feature mappings. Throughout this course, we tend to use:\n"
"* $\\Phi(\\vec{x})$ when discussing feature mappings in machine learning, generally, and\n"
"* $U(\\vec{x})$ when discussing circuit implementations of feature mappings.\n"
msgstr ""
"Tambahan pula, adalah lazim menggunakan simbol $\\Phi(\\vec{x})$ untuk merujuk kepada pemetaan ciri $\\Phi$ bagi vektor data $\\vec{x}$. Dalam pengkomputeran kuantum khususnya, adalah lazim merujuk kepada pemetaan dalam pengkomputeran kuantum menggunakan $U(\\vec{x}),$ satu tatatanda yang menekankan sifat unitari operasi tersebut. Seseorang boleh menggunakan simbol yang sama untuk kedua-duanya; keduanya adalah pemetaan ciri. Sepanjang kursus ini, kami cenderung menggunakan:\n"
"* $\\Phi(\\vec{x})$ apabila membincangkan pemetaan ciri dalam pembelajaran mesin secara umum, dan\n"
"* $U(\\vec{x})$ apabila membincangkan pelaksanaan litar bagi pemetaan ciri.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Normalization and information loss {#normalization-and-information-loss}"
msgstr "Penormalan dan kehilangan maklumat {#normalization-and-information-loss}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In classical machine learning, training data features are often \"normalized\" or rescaled which often improves model performance. One common way of doing this is by using min-max normalization or standardization. In min-max normalization, feature columns of the data matrix $\\text{X}$ (say, feature $k$) are normalized:\n"
msgstr "Dalam pembelajaran mesin klasik, ciri data latihan sering \"dinormalkan\" atau ditingkatkan semula skalanya, yang sering meningkatkan prestasi model. Satu cara lazim melakukan ini adalah dengan menggunakan penormalan min-maks atau piawai. Dalam penormalan min-maks, lajur ciri matriks data $\\text{X}$ (katakan, ciri $k$) dinormalkan:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"x^{'(i)}_k = \\frac{x^{(i)}_k - \\text{min}\\{x^{(j)}_k\\,|\\,\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\in [\\text{X}]\\}}{\\text{max}\\{x^{(j)}_k\\,|\\,\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\in [\\text{X}]\\}-\\text{min}\\{x^{(j)}_k\\,|\\,\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\in [\\text{X}]\\}}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"x^{'(i)}_k = \\frac{x^{(i)}_k - \\text{min}\\{x^{(j)}_k\\,|\\,\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\in [\\text{X}]\\}}{\\text{max}\\{x^{(j)}_k\\,|\\,\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\in [\\text{X}]\\}-\\text{min}\\{x^{(j)}_k\\,|\\,\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\in [\\text{X}]\\}}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where min and max refer to the minimum and maximum of feature $k$ over the $M$ data vectors in the dataset $\\text{X}$. All the feature values then fall in the unit interval: $x^{'(i)}_k \\in [0,1]$ for all $i\\in [M]$, $k\\in[N]$.\n"
msgstr "di mana min dan maks merujuk kepada minimum dan maksimum ciri $k$ merentasi $M$ vektor data dalam set data $\\text{X}$. Semua nilai ciri kemudian jatuh dalam selang unit: $x^{'(i)}_k \\in [0,1]$ untuk semua $i\\in [M]$, $k\\in[N]$.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Normalization is also a fundamental concept in quantum mechanics and quantum computing, but it is slightly different from min-max normalization. Normalization in quantum mechanics requires that the length (in the context of quantum computing, the 2-norm) of a state vector $|\\psi\\rangle$ is equal to unity: $\\|\\psi\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle} = 1$, ensuring that measurement probabilities sum to 1. The state is normalized by dividing by the 2-norm; that is, by rescaling\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle\\rightarrow\\|\\psi\\|^{-1}|\\psi\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
"In quantum computing and quantum mechanics, this is not a normalization imposed by people on the data, but a fundamental property of quantum states. Depending on your encoding scheme, this constraint may affect how your data are rescaled. For example, in amplitude encoding (see below), the data vector is normalized $\\vert\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\vert = 1$ as is required by quantum mechanics, and this affects the scaling of the data being encoded. In phase encoding, feature values are recommended to be rescaled as $\\vec{x}^{(j)}_i \\in (0,2\\pi]$ so that there is no information loss due to the modulo-$2\\pi$ effect of encoding to a qubit phase angle[\\[1,2\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""
"Penormalan juga merupakan konsep asas dalam mekanik kuantum dan pengkomputeran kuantum, tetapi ia sedikit berbeza dari penormalan min-maks. Penormalan dalam mekanik kuantum memerlukan panjang (dalam konteks pengkomputeran kuantum, norma-2) vektor keadaan $|\\psi\\rangle$ bersamaan dengan satu: $\\|\\psi\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle} = 1$, memastikan kebarangkalian pengukuran berjumlah 1. Keadaan dinormalkan dengan membahagi dengan norma-2; iaitu dengan menskal semula\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle\\rightarrow\\|\\psi\\|^{-1}|\\psi\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
"Dalam pengkomputeran kuantum dan mekanik kuantum, ini bukan penormalan yang dikenakan oleh manusia ke atas data, tetapi sifat asas keadaan kuantum. Bergantung kepada skim pengekodan anda, kekangan ini mungkin mempengaruhi cara data anda diskalakan semula. Contohnya, dalam pengekodan amplitud (lihat di bawah), vektor data dinormalkan $\\vert\\vec{x}^{(j)}\\vert = 1$ seperti yang dikehendaki oleh mekanik kuantum, dan ini mempengaruhi penskalaan data yang dikodkan. Dalam pengekodan fasa, nilai ciri disyorkan untuk diskalakan semula sebagai $\\vec{x}^{(j)}_i \\in (0,2\\pi]$ supaya tiada kehilangan maklumat akibat kesan modulo-$2\\pi$ daripada pengekodan ke sudut fasa qubit[\\[1,2\\]](#references).\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Methods of encoding {#methods-of-encoding}"
msgstr "Kaedah pengekodan {#methods-of-encoding}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the next few sections, we will refer to a small example classical dataset $\\text{X}_\\text{ex}$ consisting of $M=5$ data vectors, each with $N=3$ features:\n"
msgstr "Dalam beberapa bahagian seterusnya, kami akan merujuk kepada contoh set data klasik kecil $\\text{X}_\\text{ex}$ yang terdiri daripada $M=5$ vektor data, masing-masing dengan $N=3$ ciri:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\text{X}_{\\text{ex}}=\\{(4,8,5),(9,8,6),(2,9,2),(5,7,0),(3,7,5)\\}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\text{X}_{\\text{ex}}=\\{(4,8,5),(9,8,6),(2,9,2),(5,7,0),(3,7,5)\\}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the notation introduced above, we might say the $1^\\text{st}$ feature of the $4^\\text{th}$ data vector in our set $\\text{X}_{\\text{ex}}$ is $\\vec{x}^{(4)}_1 = 5,$ for example.\n"
msgstr "Dalam tatatanda yang diperkenalkan di atas, kita mungkin berkata ciri ke-$1$ dari vektor data ke-$4$ dalam set $\\text{X}_{\\text{ex}}$ ialah $\\vec{x}^{(4)}_1 = 5,$ sebagai contoh.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Basis encoding {#basis-encoding}"
msgstr "Pengekodan basis {#basis-encoding}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Basis encoding encodes a classical $P$-bit string into a computational basis state of a $P$-qubit system. Take for example $\\vec{x}^{(1)}_3 = 5 = 0(2^3)+1(2^2)+0(2^1)+1(2^0).$ This can be represented as a $4$-bit string as $(0101)$, and by a $4$-qubit system as the quantum state $|0101\\rangle$. More generally, for a $P$-bit string: $\\vec{x}^{(j)}_k = (b_1, b_2, ... , b_P)$, the corresponding $P$-qubit state is $|x^{(j)}_k\\rangle = | b_1, b_2, ... , b_P \\rangle$ with $b_n \\in \\{0,1\\}$ for $n = 1 , \\dots , P$. Note that this is just for a single feature.\n"
msgstr "Pengekodan basis mengekodkan rentetan $P$-bit klasik ke dalam keadaan basis pengiraan sistem $P$-qubit. Ambil contoh $\\vec{x}^{(1)}_3 = 5 = 0(2^3)+1(2^2)+0(2^1)+1(2^0).$ Ini boleh diwakili sebagai rentetan $4$-bit iaitu $(0101)$, dan oleh sistem $4$-qubit sebagai keadaan kuantum $|0101\\rangle$. Secara lebih umum, untuk rentetan $P$-bit: $\\vec{x}^{(j)}_k = (b_1, b_2, ... , b_P)$, keadaan $P$-qubit yang sepadan ialah $|x^{(j)}_k\\rangle = | b_1, b_2, ... , b_P \\rangle$ dengan $b_n \\in \\{0,1\\}$ untuk $n = 1 , \\dots , P$. Perhatikan bahawa ini hanya untuk satu ciri sahaja.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Basis encoding in quantum computing represents each classical bit as a separate qubit, mapping the binary representation of data directly onto quantum states in the computational basis. When multiple features need to be encoded, each feature is first converted to its binary form and then assigned to a distinct group of qubits — one group per feature — where each qubit reflects a bit in the binary representation of that feature.\n"
msgstr "Pengekodan basis dalam pengkomputeran kuantum mewakili setiap bit klasik sebagai qubit berasingan, memetakan perwakilan binari data terus ke keadaan kuantum dalam basis pengiraan. Apabila pelbagai ciri perlu dikodkan, setiap ciri pertama ditukar ke bentuk binarinya, kemudian diberikan kepada kumpulan qubit yang berlainan — satu kumpulan bagi setiap ciri — di mana setiap qubit mencerminkan satu bit dalam perwakilan binari ciri tersebut.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As an example, let us encode the vector (5, 7, 0).\n"
msgstr "Sebagai contoh, mari kita kodkan vektor (5, 7, 0).\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose all features are stored in four bits (more than we need, but enough to represent any integer that is single-digit in base 10):\n"
msgstr "Andaikan semua ciri disimpan dalam empat bit (lebih dari yang diperlukan, tetapi cukup untuk mewakili mana-mana integer digit tunggal dalam asas 10):\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "    5 → binary 0101\n"
msgstr "    5 → binary 0101\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "    7 → binary 0111\n"
msgstr "    7 → binary 0111\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "    0 → binary 0000\n"
msgstr "    0 → binary 0000\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"These bit strings are assigned to three sets of four qubits, so the overall 12-qubit basis state is:\n"
"$$\n"
"∣0101 0111 0000⟩\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Rentetan bit ini diberikan kepada tiga set empat qubit, jadi keseluruhan keadaan basis 12-qubit ialah:\n"
"$$\n"
"∣0101 0111 0000⟩\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here, the first four qubits represent the first feature, the next four qubits the second feature, and the last four qubits the third feature. The code below converts the data vector (5,7,0) to a quantum state, and is generalized to do so for other single-digit features.\n"
msgstr "Di sini, empat qubit pertama mewakili ciri pertama, empat qubit berikutnya mewakili ciri kedua, dan empat qubit terakhir mewakili ciri ketiga. Kod di bawah menukar vektor data (5,7,0) kepada keadaan kuantum, dan digeneralisasikan untuk berbuat demikian bagi ciri digit tunggal yang lain.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/quantum-machine-learning/data-encoding/extracted-outputs/85ee995f-1e50-4860-a24c-16bbc8b5c8b0-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/quantum-machine-learning/data-encoding/extracted-outputs/85ee995f-1e50-4860-a24c-16bbc8b5c8b0-0.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "Uji kefahaman anda {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Write code to encode the first vector in our example data set $\\text{X}_{\\text{ex}}$:\n"
msgstr "Tulis kod untuk mengekodkan vektor pertama dalam set data contoh $\\text{X}_{\\text{ex}}$:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$\\vec{x}^{(1)}=(4,8,5)$$\n"
msgstr "$$\\vec{x}^{(1)}=(4,8,5)$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "using basis encoding.\n"
msgstr "menggunakan pengekodan basis.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Jawapan\">\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Amplitude encoding {#amplitude-encoding}"
msgstr "Pengekodan amplitud {#amplitude-encoding}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Amplitude encoding encodes data into the amplitudes of a quantum state. It represents a normalized classical $N$-dimensional data vector, $\\vec{x}^{(j)}$, as the amplitudes of a $n$-qubit quantum state, $|\\psi_x\\rangle$:\n"
msgstr "Pengekodan amplitud mengekodkan data ke dalam amplitud keadaan kuantum. Ia mewakili vektor data klasik $N$-dimensi yang dinormalkan, $\\vec{x}^{(j)}$, sebagai amplitud keadaan kuantum $n$-qubit, $|\\psi_x\\rangle$:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi^{(j)}_x\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\alpha}\\sum_{i=1}^N x^{(j)}_i |i\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi^{(j)}_x\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\alpha}\\sum_{i=1}^N x^{(j)}_i |i\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where $N$ is the same dimension of the data vectors as before, $\\vec{x}^{(j)}_i$ is the $i^{th}$ element of $\\vec{x}^{(j)}$ and $|i\\rangle$ is the $i^{th}$ computational basis state. Here, $\\alpha$ is a normalization constant to be determined from the data being encoded. This is the normalization condition imposed by quantum mechanics:\n"
msgstr "di mana $N$ ialah dimensi vektor data yang sama seperti sebelumnya, $\\vec{x}^{(j)}_i$ ialah elemen ke-$i$ bagi $\\vec{x}^{(j)}$ dan $|i\\rangle$ ialah keadaan basis pengiraan ke-$i$. Di sini, $\\alpha$ ialah pemalar penormalan yang ditentukan daripada data yang dikodkan. Ini adalah syarat penormalan yang dikenakan oleh mekanik kuantum:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{i=1}^N \\left|x^{(j)}_i\\right|^2 = \\left|\\alpha\\right|^2.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{i=1}^N \\left|x^{(j)}_i\\right|^2 = \\left|\\alpha\\right|^2.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In general, this is a different condition than the min/max normalization used for each feature across all data vectors. Precisely how this is navigated will depend on your problem. But there is no way around the quantum mechanical normalization condition above.\n"
msgstr "Secara umum, ini adalah syarat yang berbeza daripada penormalan min/maks yang digunakan untuk setiap ciri merentasi semua vektor data. Cara menangani ini akan bergantung kepada masalah anda. Tetapi tiada cara untuk mengelakkan syarat penormalan mekanik kuantum di atas.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In amplitude encoding, each feature in a data vector is stored as an amplitude of a different quantum state. As a system of $n$ qubits provides $2^n$ amplitudes, amplitude encoding of $N$ features requires $n \\ge \\mathrm{log}_2(N)$ qubits.\n"
msgstr "Dalam pengekodan amplitud, setiap ciri dalam vektor data disimpan sebagai amplitud keadaan kuantum yang berbeza. Memandangkan sistem $n$ qubit menyediakan $2^n$ amplitud, pengekodan amplitud bagi $N$ ciri memerlukan $n \\ge \\mathrm{log}_2(N)$ qubit.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As an example, let's encode the first vector in our example dataset $\\text{X}_\\text{ex}$, $\\vec{x}^{(1)} = (4,8,5)$ using amplitude encoding. Normalizing the resulting vector, we get:\n"
msgstr "Sebagai contoh, mari kita kodkan vektor pertama dalam set data contoh $\\text{X}_\\text{ex}$, $\\vec{x}^{(1)} = (4,8,5)$ menggunakan pengekodan amplitud. Dengan menormalkan vektor yang terhasil, kita dapat:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{i=1}^N \\left|x^{(1)}_i\\right|^2 = 4^2+8^2+5^2 = 105 = \\left|\\alpha\\right|^2 \\rightarrow \\alpha = \\sqrt{105}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\sum_{i=1}^N \\left|x^{(1)}_i\\right|^2 = 4^2+8^2+5^2 = 105 = \\left|\\alpha\\right|^2 \\rightarrow \\alpha = \\sqrt{105}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "and the resulting 2-qubit quantum state would be:\n"
msgstr "dan keadaan kuantum 2-qubit yang terhasil ialah:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(\\vec{x}^{(1)})\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{105}}(4|00\\rangle+8|01\\rangle+5|10\\rangle+0|11\\rangle)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(\\vec{x}^{(1)})\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{105}}(4|00\\rangle+8|01\\rangle+5|10\\rangle+0|11\\rangle)\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the example above, the number of features in the vector $N=3$, is not a power of 2. When $N$ is not a power of 2, we simply choose a value for the number of qubits $n$ such that $2^n\\geq N$ and pad the amplitude vector with uninformative constants (here, a zero).\n"
msgstr "Dalam contoh di atas, bilangan ciri dalam vektor $N=3$, bukan kuasa 2. Apabila $N$ bukan kuasa 2, kita hanya memilih nilai untuk bilangan qubit $n$ supaya $2^n\\geq N$ dan mengisi vektor amplitud dengan pemalar tidak bermaklumat (di sini, sifar).\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Like in basis encoding, once we calculate what state will encode our dataset, in Qiskit we can use the `initialize` function to prepare it:\n"
msgstr "Seperti dalam pengekodan basis, setelah kita mengira keadaan yang akan mengekodkan set data kita, dalam Qiskit kita boleh menggunakan fungsi `initialize` untuk menyediakannya:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/quantum-machine-learning/data-encoding/extracted-outputs/19810c6d-8d60-49ee-bd6f-6f6fbd5e7363-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/quantum-machine-learning/data-encoding/extracted-outputs/19810c6d-8d60-49ee-bd6f-6f6fbd5e7363-0.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "An advantage of amplitude encoding is the aforementioned requirement of only $\\mathrm{log}_2(N)$ qubits to encode. However, subsequent algorithms must operate on the amplitudes of a quantum state, and methods to prepare and measure the quantum states tend not to be efficient.\n"
msgstr "Kelebihan pengekodan amplitud ialah keperluan hanya $\\mathrm{log}_2(N)$ qubit untuk mengekodkan. Walau bagaimanapun, algoritma seterusnya mesti beroperasi ke atas amplitud keadaan kuantum, dan kaedah untuk menyediakan serta mengukur keadaan kuantum cenderung tidak cekap.\n"

